#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3f7d789-7fe7-56e3-a007-c3a764e6bcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3f7d789-7fe7-56e3-a007-c3a764e6bcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374997} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e57f55-2229-5b7f-9155-1498080145f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d208a446-252e-5962-98f7-431e5b98dc76" :question/math-academy-id "q-168615" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43198aae-a4dd-5b79-803a-1bb625a96355" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Volume of a Solid Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa80643-af7b-58ec-b9e6-62aa28b5ef73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa80643-af7b-58ec-b9e6-62aa28b5ef73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375000} :answer-field/id #uuid "67229e62-dd53-573b-adcf-192d051bb308" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f84fc6b5-0699-5ceb-9dc7-5f701d2d5fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-168616" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/dc432b01077dc153940a88689f398283c09ee2a9d3385f4619a2e4a12cfa9c10.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/fcb2a304b7333a8ee3d2ae38c26b337fa4e486f0d33034b68982dc9f2cc0bb11.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(4-x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((4-x)-\\frac{1}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{7}{2}-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd220b49-a468-509f-b367-91fe81f808b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd220b49-a468-509f-b367-91fe81f808b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375003} :answer-field/id #uuid "90629955-3091-5dbc-8451-60186f068b6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "768c2a7d-6a51-5200-ac90-d2b7874a059f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168617" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f44e99a-00a9-5df9-bf70-c3e9b14260fa" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea868574-78ae-585e-971a-a3dae1771c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea868574-78ae-585e-971a-a3dae1771c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375006} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ffd7db-1548-5621-80f2-3165aabe6ed3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aed15fbe-5e59-5d92-bf7c-ed947204cd89" :question/math-academy-id "q-168618" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x(4-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[8xy-2{x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}7x-2{x}^{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a622775d-a79f-57fc-86c6-e42336367b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a622775d-a79f-57fc-86c6-e42336367b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375009} :answer-field/id #uuid "caabcbe7-e42a-536e-969c-a619ef52c6ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375008} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9627f583-6aa3-5ce2-b369-e20efe21cf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9627f583-6aa3-5ce2-b369-e20efe21cf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2z-y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375011} :answer-field/id #uuid "25e41b5d-78dd-5d5c-a61a-90b2ff053c93" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c44f28b-b161-58d2-b2dd-b96c7d73145a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c44f28b-b161-58d2-b2dd-b96c7d73145a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375013} :answer-field/id #uuid "05cb673c-c2ec-53d3-97a6-0dffdc671a25" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12d221b0-8d48-5bff-9a95-221cf2846fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-168621" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+(2y-0)\\,k \\\\  & =2xy\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+2y\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7bc637a-6068-5d79-953d-64455b3cbd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-2x+2xy-y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7bc637a-6068-5d79-953d-64455b3cbd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-2x+2xy-y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375016} :answer-field/id #uuid "977e44ef-f1ba-589c-8034-63e32f6d1656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2835fb6e-1491-5ec3-8845-9b31e74ebdbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-168625" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2xy$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2z-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required circulation is ${∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2xy}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16601513-9b74-5cec-9af6-2d99b98e1ab3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a632af-4cb5-52d4-abef-f6e5a8faf034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73a632af-4cb5-52d4-abef-f6e5a8faf034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375019} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa962c53-d8c5-5cd7-9e4b-a23355e805a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c010048-751c-55ee-bc2d-866d3ad0598c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168626" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2xy$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2z-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nLet $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required circulation is ${∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8-2x+2xy-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x{y}^{2}-2xy-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+8y]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2x-\\frac{1}{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{23}{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{23}{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{23x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
